{"text": "6120\n\nReceptor-mediated Endothelial Cell Dysfunction in Diabetic Vasculopathy\n\u2018Soluble Receptor for Advanced Glycation End Products Blocks Hyperpermeability in Diabetic Rats\n\nDysfunctional endothelium is associated with and, likely,\npredates clinical complications of diabetes mellitus, by pro-\nmoting increased vascular permeability and thrombogenic-\nity. Irreversible advanced glycation end products (AGEs),\nresulting from nonenzymatic glycation and oxidation of\nProteins or lipids, are found in plasma, vessel wall, and tis-\nSues and have been linked to the development of diabetic\ncomplications. The principal means through which AGEs\nexert their cellular effects is via specific cellular receptors.\none of which. receptor for AGE (RAGE). is expressed by\ndothelium. We report that blockade of RAGE. inhibits\nAGE-induced impairment of endothelial barrier function,\nand reverses, in large part, the early vascular hyperperme-\nability observed in diabetic rats. Inhibition of AGE- and dia-\nbetes-mediated hyperpermeability by antioxidants, both in\nvitro and in vivo, suggested the central role of AGE-RAGE-\ninduced oxidant stress in the development of hyperperme-\nility. Taken together, these data support the concept that\nation of AGEs by endothelial RAGE induces cellular dys-\nfunction, at least in part by an oxidant-sensitive mecha- ;\nnism, contributing to vascular hyperpermeability in diabe- it\ntes, and that RAGE is central to this pathologic process. Paar,\n\n\u2018Wautier, J.-L., Zoukourian, C., Chappey, O., Wautier, M.-P., Guillausseau, P.-J.,\nCao, R., Hori, O., Stern, D., and Schmidt, A.M.\n\nJournal of Clinical Investigation 97(1):238-243, January 1996.\n\nOther support: U.S. Public Health Service, Juvenile Diabetes Foundation, New York\nAffiliate of the American Heart Association, and the Surgical Research Fund.\n\nFrom the Unite d'Immunohematologie and Departments of Internal Medicine,\nLaboratorie de Recherche en Biologie Vasculaire et Cellulaire, Paris, France and\nDepartment of Physiology, Medicine and Surgery, Columbia University, College of\nPhysicians and Surgeons, New York, NY.\n\nPUBLICATIONS 005427.",
